{ [%anno "jomega", jomega] [Last Modified: 8/27/2025]
-
This study is the top level of a collection of studies on the topic of "Chess Structures", or what is also called "Pawn Structure Chess".

A study of chess structures is aimed at providing a strategic guide to the most frequently occurring classes of positions in the openings. Those positions are based on the pawn structures that occur.

For a formation of pawns to be considered part of a particular named "structure", it need not have a pawn position identical to the corresponding diagram. The formation of the pawns need only be close enough that the major themes are unchanged.

The same formation of pawns with colors reversed is also considered the same structure; with the White and Black plans reversed.

Pawn structures can transpose into one another. Such transpositions must be studied as they often change the plans for both sides.

Obviously, the exact position that occurs in some game, including whose move it is, will determine the viability of the plans.

There are many sources for this topic. I have gone through many of those sources and completed a comparative analysis. I believe that one of the best of the sources is "Chess Structures: A Grandmaster Guide", by Mauricio Flores Rios.

In this collection of studies you will find every structure that Flores Rios presents in his book. You will also find all the games and exercises from the book. Most of those games are from the 21st century.

I have also included three structures from the other sources that were not in Flores Rios. Every other structure from the other sources is in Fores Rios. Indeed, many of those sources used Flores Rios as a reference.

I previously tackled this topic years ago in my Intermediate Course on Lichess. That was a more gentle introduction to the topic. At that time, I based my course on the book "Pawn Structure Chess" by Andrew Soltis, 1976, and the Wikipedia page on the topic at the time I created the course. The games I used as examples for the Intermediate Course were from the 20th century. See the References chapter in this study for links to my course, and also a list of the sources I used.

These studies are "bare bones"; meaning that there are no comments in the main Lichess chapters, and no annotations in the games. This was done so that you could clone the studies and do with them as you please.

I plan to clone these studies myself and that is where I will include such things as the themes for the structures, and my own annotations on the games.

The Lichess chapters 3-5 in this study are quotes from [FLORESRIOS]. They explain what the topic "Chess Structures" is about, the families of structures as organized by Flores Rios, and his reasons for the structure choices he made.

The supporting games from [FLORESRIOS] are in 4 studies
starting here:
Chess Structures: Games and Positions: Chapters 1-8
https://lichess.org/study/AxW3sYhA

The exercises from [FLORESRIOS] are here:
Chess Structures: Exercises: Chapter 23
https://lichess.org/study/PrxEvNOQ

Also see a series of corresponding studies:
Chess Structures: Opening Connections
https://lichess.org/study/CTycB4Hi
----------
I have written many Lichess studies. All my studies are cross linked. Here is a table of contents for all my studies.
Jomega Studies Table of Contents
https://lichess.org/study/ac6JRqJk }
 *


