{ [%anno "jomega", jomega] The Structure Names
-
In my research on the names people have given to these structures, I found a number of issues.

- The names are not always descriptive of the structure.
- Different names are sometimes used for the same structure.

The reasons that these things are issues:
a) when trying to communicate with someone a common understanding of what the names means is necessary, and
b) making the names be descriptive would be a help in remembering what the structure is.

By "descriptive" I mean something in the name that would be a clue as to what the structure entailed.

An example of a name that is descriptive is "Hanging Pawns". Once one knows the definition of hanging pawns, the name is very descriptive.

As an example of a non descriptive name we have "Najdorf Type I". If one knows the Najdorf variation of the Sicilian Defense, then this is somewhat descriptive. However, the "Type I" and "Type II" extensions on the names is not a standard, and I have only found it in Flores Rios.

Because of that, I added to the Lichess chapter name the status of the main White pawns; assuming that one would find Najdorf descriptive of the Black pawns. Hence, arriving at the Lichess chapter name "Najdorf Type I: White d-pawn, no White e-pawn".

As examples of different names being used for the same structure, we have "Carlsbad", which is also known as "Orthodox Exchange", and the "Open KID", which is also known as the "Boleslavsky Wall". }
 *


